MVM.registerModule({
  "id": "m13",
  "summary": "Recognise hybrid and electric vehicles, name their main components, understand high-voltage dangers and the limits of what an untrained technician may do, and carry out safe general servicing.",
  "outcomes": ["Describe mild, full, plug-in hybrid and battery electric vehicles and recognise one in the workshop.","Name the main components of an electrified drivetrain and what each does.","Explain why high voltage is dangerous, who may work on it and the steps of an isolation procedure in outline.","Describe battery chemistries, state of charge and health, charging levels, and the risks of a damaged battery.","Explain regenerative braking and why brakes on an electrified vehicle still need service.","Describe the work that an ordinary workshop may do on these vehicles and when to refer to a specialist."],
  "cheatsheet": ["High-voltage cables are orange. Do not touch, cut or open them without training and isolation.","Mild hybrid: no electric-only drive. Full hybrid: can. Plug-in: charge port. Battery electric: no engine.","Isolation outline: ignition off, remove service disconnect, wait the set time, verify it is dead.","Use insulated gloves and tools rated for the voltage, and inspect them before each use.","Never work on a high-voltage system unless you are trained, certified and authorised.","A damaged lithium-ion battery can overheat or catch fire (thermal runaway). Keep it away from buildings and report it.","Regenerative braking uses the motor as a generator to slow the vehicle and recover energy.","Tow an electric vehicle on a flatbed unless the maker's instructions say otherwise.","The 12 V battery still runs the lights, computers and contactors. A flat 12 V battery can stop an electric vehicle from starting."],
  "lessons": [
    {"id": "m13-l1", "title": "Types of electrified vehicles", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"More and more vehicles use an electric motor, either to help the engine or to replace it. As a technician, you must be able to tell them apart, because each has different safety needs."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"hybrid-architectures"},
      {"t":"h","text":"The main types"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","How it works","Can drive on electricity alone?"],"rows":[["Mild hybrid (MHEV)","A small motor-generator, often on 48 V, assists the engine and recovers energy","No"],["Full hybrid (HEV)","A motor and a larger battery can drive the vehicle alone at low speed, and also assist the engine","Yes, for short distances"],["Plug-in hybrid (PHEV)","Larger battery that can be charged from the mains, with an engine as a back-up","Yes, for a longer distance"],["Battery electric (BEV)","Battery and electric motor only. No engine or fuel tank","Yes, always"],["Fuel-cell electric (FCEV)","A fuel cell makes electricity from hydrogen, and a motor drives the wheels","Yes"]],"caption":"Types of electrified vehicles"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Benefits and limits"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Electrified vehicles use less fuel and produce fewer emissions, and an electric motor gives strong torque from rest and quiet running. The limits are the cost and weight of the battery, charging time and availability, and the need for special training and equipment to service them. In many countries, charging stations and trained technicians are still limited, so these vehicles are mostly found in larger cities and as imports."},
      {"t":"h","text":"How to recognise one"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Badges on the body, such as Hybrid, Plug-in, EV or a similar label.","A charging port on the body of a plug-in or electric vehicle.","The engine may stop and start by itself, or the vehicle may be silent when ready to drive. A **READY** light on the dash shows that it can move.","**Orange cables** and warning labels under the bonnet and under the floor.","The VIN and the manual confirm the type."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Units you will see"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Unit","Meaning","Example"],"rows":[["kW","Power, how fast energy is used or supplied","A motor of 100 kW"],["kWh","Energy, how much is stored. 1 kWh is 1 kW for 1 hour.","A battery of 53 kWh"],["Wh/km or kWh/100 km","Energy use over a distance. Like fuel consumption.","16 kWh/100 km"],["Range","Distance a full battery gives","About 300 km"],["C-rate","How fast a battery is charged or discharged compared with its capacity","1C empties it in an hour"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"These terms tell you what a vehicle is capable of. A larger kWh figure means more range. A larger kW figure means more power or faster charging. A hybrid with a 1.3 kWh battery cannot go far on electricity, but a plug-in hybrid with 12 kWh can do a typical daily commute."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Used imports and local conditions"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In many countries, the first hybrids a workshop sees are used imports, often a few years old. They may have been built for another market, with a different battery warranty and different service information. Spare parts and trained technicians may be scarce. A replacement high-voltage battery is a major cost, sometimes a large part of the value of a used car, so the battery's condition matters a great deal when you advise on a purchase."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Check that the vehicle has a service record and that the hybrid warning lights work and go out.","Look at the **cooling inlet** of the battery, often under a rear seat. Blocked air passages or dust cause overheating and early wear in air-cooled packs.","Heat is hard on batteries, so in hot climates keep the vehicle in the shade where possible, and keep the cooling air passages clean.","Explain honestly to the customer which work your workshop can do and which must go to a specialist."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"A hybrid in the READY state may start its engine without warning, and an electric vehicle may move silently. Always switch the vehicle off, take the key away from the cabin and be sure that it is not in READY before working near the engine or under the vehicle."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Treat any vehicle with orange cables as a high-voltage vehicle until you have confirmed the type from the manual."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m13-l2", "title": "Main components", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Electrified vehicles share a set of main parts. Knowing where they are and what they do lets you avoid danger and understand faults."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"ev-powertrain"},
      {"t":"h","text":"High-voltage battery"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **traction battery** stores the energy for driving. It is made of many cells grouped into modules inside a protective, sealed housing, usually under the floor or the rear seat. Its voltage is typically from about 100 V to over 800 V, depending on the vehicle. It has a **battery management system (BMS)** that monitors the cells' voltage, temperature and charge, and it switches the battery on and off through **contactors** (heavy relays)."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Inverter, motor and generator"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An **inverter** changes the battery's direct current (DC) to alternating current (AC) for the motor, and controls the motor's speed and torque. Most motors are AC machines. The same machine can work as a **generator** when the vehicle slows, sending energy back to the battery through the inverter."},
      {"t":"h","text":"DC-DC converter and on-board charger"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**DC-DC converter:** lowers the high voltage to 12 V to charge the 12 V battery and run the normal vehicle systems, doing the job of an alternator.","**On-board charger:** changes AC from the mains to DC at the voltage that the battery needs, when the vehicle is plugged in.","**Thermal management:** pumps, coolant circuits, heaters and sometimes the A/C refrigerant system keep the battery and power electronics at the right temperature."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The 12 V auxiliary battery"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Electrified vehicles still have a 12 V battery. It powers the control modules, lights, locks and the contactors that connect the high-voltage battery. If the 12 V battery is flat, the vehicle may not start, even when the high-voltage battery is fully charged. Many faults on electrified vehicles are caused by a weak 12 V battery."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Cables and labels"},
      {"t":"p","text":"High-voltage cables are covered in **orange** insulation, and the parts that carry high voltage have warning labels. Orange cables must never be cut, pierced or removed by anyone who is not trained."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Contactors and pre-charge"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The high-voltage battery connects to the rest of the system through heavy relays called **contactors**, switched by the battery control unit. When the vehicle is switched on, the unit first closes a small **pre-charge** contactor through a resistor, which fills the capacitors of the inverter slowly. Only when the voltage has risen to nearly battery voltage does it close the main contactors. This prevents a huge surge of current. When the vehicle is switched off, or when a fault or crash is detected, the contactors open, and the high-voltage circuit outside the battery is **discharged**."},
      {"t":"p","text":"A fault in a contactor, a pre-charge circuit, the control unit or the 12 V supply to them may stop the vehicle from going READY, with warnings on the dash. These are jobs for a trained technician with the right tools, because the work involves the high-voltage battery."},
      {"t":"h","text":"High-voltage interlock and insulation monitoring"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Safety systems watch the high-voltage circuit all the time. A **high-voltage interlock loop** (HVIL) is a thin low-voltage wire that runs through every high-voltage connector and cover. If a connector is unplugged, or a cover removed, the loop opens, and the control unit opens the contactors and discharges the system. An **insulation monitor** measures the resistance between each high-voltage line and the vehicle body, several times a second. It looks for leakage that could shock someone. If the resistance drops below the safe limit, the system sets an **isolation fault** and may refuse to start or may shut down."},
      {"t":"p","text":"These systems protect people, but they are not a reason to take chances. A vehicle that has set an isolation fault, perhaps from water in a charge port, a damaged cable or a failing motor winding, must be referred to a trained technician. Never reset such a fault and carry on."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A high-voltage system is isolated from the vehicle body. A fault that connects either side to the body is detected by the system, and may disable the vehicle, but it can still be dangerous."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Look at the layout diagram in the manual before you start any work. It shows the places that the orange cables run and the position of the service disconnect."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m13-l3", "title": "Traction motors and power electronics", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"An electric motor is simpler than an engine, with far fewer moving parts. It makes full torque from rest, and it needs no gearbox with many speeds. But it depends on power electronics that are as complex as the engine it replaces. This lesson explains how a traction motor works, the shape of its torque curve, and what the inverter does. It describes how the parts work so that you can understand a vehicle. It does not mean you may work on high-voltage parts without training."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Motor types"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","How it works","Features"],"rows":[["Permanent-magnet synchronous (PMSM)","Magnets in the rotor lock onto a rotating field made by the stator coils","High efficiency and power density. Most common in cars. Uses rare-earth magnets."],["Induction (asynchronous)","The rotor has no magnets. The stator field induces current in the rotor, which follows the field with a small slip.","Robust and cheaper. A little less efficient at light load."],["Wound-rotor synchronous","The rotor has coils supplied with current","No magnets, good control. Used in some makes."],["Switched reluctance","Iron teeth are pulled toward energised coils","Simple and strong. Noisier. Rare in cars."]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"A motor has a **stator**, the fixed outer part with three-phase windings, and a **rotor** on the shaft. The inverter feeds the stator three alternating currents, each shifted a third of a turn, so the magnetic field rotates. The rotor follows it. A **resolver** or other position sensor on the shaft tells the inverter where the rotor is, so that it can time the currents exactly. A fault in the sensor or its wiring stops the motor, or makes it run roughly."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The torque and power curve"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A motor gives its maximum torque from zero speed up to a **base speed**. Above it, the voltage limit is reached, and the torque falls while the power stays constant, until the top speed. This is why an electric vehicle accelerates strongly from rest but does not push so hard at high speed."},
      {"t":"example","title":"A motor torque and power curve","steps":["A motor has a peak torque of 250 N·m up to a base speed of 4,000 rpm.","Power at the base speed = 250 × 4,000 ÷ 9,549 = 104.7 kW.","Above 4,000 rpm, power is held at about 104.7 kW. At 8,000 rpm the torque is 104.7 × 9,549 ÷ 8,000 = 125 N·m, half the peak.","A single-speed gearbox with a reduction of 9:1 multiplies the torque at the wheels to 250 × 9 = 2,250 N·m at low speed (ignoring losses).","At 8,000 rpm, the wheels turn at 8,000 ÷ 9 = 889 rpm. With a rolling circumference of 1.985 m, the speed is 889 × 1.985 × 60 ÷ 1,000 = 106 km/h."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The inverter"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The inverter changes the battery's DC into three-phase AC. It uses six groups of power switches, **IGBTs** or silicon carbide **MOSFETs**, that turn on and off tens of thousands of times a second. By changing the width of the pulses (PWM), it makes an alternating current of the frequency and size needed. A higher frequency makes the motor spin faster, and a bigger current gives more torque. The same switches let current flow back from the motor when the vehicle slows, so the motor acts as a generator."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Inverter part","Job"],"rows":[["Power switches (IGBT or SiC)","Switch the battery voltage onto each motor phase"],["DC link capacitor","Smooths the battery voltage. Holds energy after the vehicle is off, so it needs time to discharge."],["Gate drivers and controller","Command the switches according to the torque request"],["Current sensors","Report the motor currents for control and protection"],["Cooling plate","Carries heat away. The inverter has its own coolant circuit."]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Efficiency and heat"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An electric drive can convert 90 to 95% of the battery's energy into movement, much better than an engine. The rest becomes heat in the motor and the inverter, which are cooled by a liquid circuit. A leak, an air lock or a failed pump in this circuit causes power to be reduced to protect the parts. Check the coolant level at the service, using the maker's coolant."},
      {"t":"h","text":"What can go wrong"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Position sensor faults:** the motor stalls or judders. A scan tool shows a resolver code.","**Insulation breakdown:** the winding insulation fails and the system detects an isolation fault.","**Bearing wear:** whining or grinding noise from the motor or gearbox. Some EVs have noise that is more obvious without engine noise.","**Overheating:** reduced power and warnings. Check the cooling circuit.","**Inverter faults:** codes for switches, current sensors or the controller. Replace the unit as the maker advises."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Electric drive has few moving parts, but its electronics are complex. Most faults are found by scan tool, and repaired by trained technicians following the maker's procedure."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"The inverter's capacitors can hold a charge after the vehicle is off. Never open an inverter or touch orange cables unless you are trained and the system has been isolated and proved dead."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m13-l4", "title": "High-voltage safety", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"This is the most important lesson in this module. High voltage can kill. It is a hazard that you can not see, hear or smell."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Why it is dangerous"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Voltages above about 60 V DC or 30 V AC can cause a lethal shock, and traction batteries run at hundreds of volts and can deliver very large currents. A shock can stop the heart and cause deep burns. The battery can not be switched off, since it is a source of energy, and some parts remain charged after the vehicle is switched off, for several minutes. A short circuit can cause an arc flash, severe burns and fire."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Training and authority"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Working on high-voltage systems needs specific **training and certification**, which vary by country and maker. A person must be **qualified and authorised** to work on a vehicle. If you are not trained, you must not touch or work on any high-voltage part, including orange cables, the battery, the inverter, the charger and the motor."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Protective equipment"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Insulating gloves** rated for the system's voltage (often Class 0, 1,000 V) with leather protectors over them. Inspect and air-test them before every use.","**Insulated tools** rated for the voltage.","**Face shield or safety glasses**, and clothes with no metal items. Remove jewellery.","**Insulating mat**, a rescue hook for rescuing someone who is being shocked, and warning signs and barriers around the vehicle."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Isolation procedure in outline"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Switch the vehicle off and remove the key or key fob well away from the vehicle.","Disconnect the 12 V battery, if the manual says so.","Wearing the protective equipment, remove the **service disconnect** (a plug or switch that breaks the high-voltage battery circuit).","Wait the time the manufacturer gives, often 5 to 10 minutes or more, so the stored energy in the system's capacitors falls.","**Verify** with a suitable meter that no dangerous voltage remains at the point you will work on.","Put a warning label and a lock on the disconnect, and keep the key with you."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"What ordinary mechanics must not do"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Never cut, open, pierce or unplug orange cables or components unless trained and authorised.","Never assume that a vehicle is safe because it is switched off.","Never work alone on a high-voltage vehicle. A second trained person must be able to act in an emergency.","Never work on a wet vehicle or standing in water."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Levels of competence"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Level","Typical scope","Who"],"rows":[["Awareness","Recognise the vehicle and its hazards. Keep clear. Know who to call and what to do in an emergency. No work on high-voltage parts.","All workshop staff, including reception, tyre fitters and body workers"],["Non-live work","Work on the vehicle after it has been isolated and proved dead by a competent person, following the maker's procedure","Trained and certified technicians"],["Live work","Test, diagnose or work on a system that cannot be switched off, with specialised equipment and procedures","Specialists with advanced training"]],"caption":"Names and requirements differ between countries and employers. The aim is the same: only competent people go near high voltage."},
      {"t":"h","text":"A safe workshop"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Mark a high-voltage work bay with signs and barriers, and keep untrained people out.","Keep insulated gloves, tools, a rescue hook, an insulating mat and a first aid kit at the bay, and a fire extinguisher suitable for the vehicle.","Keep a record of who is trained, and what each person is allowed to do.","Keep the maker's procedures at hand, and follow them every time, not from memory.","Make sure that a second person who is trained knows what to do in an emergency, including how to isolate the supply and call for help."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"If you are not trained, your part in a high-voltage job is to keep clear, to tell others of the hazard, and to call for the right person. That is a responsible and skilled thing to do. Never take a risk because a customer or a colleague is in a hurry."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"If someone is receiving a shock, do not touch them. Switch off the supply if you can, or use an insulated rescue hook to pull them clear, then call for help and give first aid."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"If you are not trained and certified, your job is to recognise the high-voltage system, keep away from it and refer the work to a specialist."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m13-l5", "title": "Battery and charging", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The battery is the most valuable and most delicate part of an electrified vehicle. This lesson explains how it is described, charged and handled."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Cell chemistries"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Nickel-metal hydride (NiMH):** used in many older hybrids. Robust, but heavier and less energy dense.","**Lithium-ion (Li-ion):** the usual choice for newer vehicles. Different types, such as NMC and LFP, vary in energy density, life and cost. They need careful temperature and voltage control."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"State of charge and state of health"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**State of charge (SoC)** is how full the battery is, shown as a percentage. **State of health (SoH)** is how much capacity the battery has left compared to when it was new. A battery fades with age and use. The vehicle computer calculates both, and a scan tool can show them. Never judge a high-voltage battery by measuring it directly."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Charging levels and connectors"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Charging type","Power supply","Typical use"],"rows":[["Level 1 (AC)","Household socket","Slow, overnight, small battery or plug-in hybrid"],["Level 2 (AC)","Wallbox or public AC point","A few hours for a full charge"],["DC fast charging","Rapid public charger, DC fed straight into the battery","Quick top-ups on long journeys"]],"caption":"Charging levels"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Connector types differ by region and maker, for example Type 1, Type 2, CCS, CHAdeMO and GB/T. Always use the right cable and charger for the vehicle. A damaged cable, plug or socket must not be used."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Thermal runaway"},
      {"t":"p","text":"If a lithium-ion cell is damaged, overcharged or shorted, it can overheat in a chain reaction called **thermal runaway**. It can release toxic gases and flames, and may re-ignite many hours or days later. Heat, crash damage, water ingress and manufacturing faults can cause it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Damaged batteries and storage"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A vehicle whose battery has been damaged, for example in a crash or flood, must be treated as a fire risk. Park it outdoors, away from buildings and other vehicles, and tell the owner and the emergency services. Do not charge it. Store a battery in a cool, dry place at the charge level the maker recommends, usually partly charged. Do not store a vehicle with a flat battery for long."},
      {"t":"h","text":"If the vehicle will not charge"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Check","Notes"],"rows":[["The supply","Try another socket or charge point. A faulty supply, a tripped breaker or a charge point error is the commonest cause."],["The cable and plug","Look for damage, burn marks and dirt in the pins. Never use a damaged cable."],["The vehicle's charge port","Look for bent pins, water or debris, and a stuck flap or lock."],["The 12 V battery","A flat 12 V battery can stop the vehicle from waking up to charge."],["Settings and timers","A charging schedule, a limit set by the owner, or a temperature limit can delay charging."],["Warnings and codes","Read the codes with a scan tool. Charger faults, communication faults and temperature faults are logged."]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Do not open the charge port wiring or the on-board charger. These carry high voltage and mains voltage. If the cause is not an easy check, refer the vehicle to a trained technician."},
      {"t":"h","text":"One-pedal driving and brake feel"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many electric vehicles have strong regeneration when the driver lifts off the accelerator. In **one-pedal driving** the car slows down so firmly that the driver can drive in town without touching the brake pedal. The brake lights come on automatically when the regeneration is strong. Customers may notice different brake pedal feel, because the car blends the motor and friction brakes. A firm pedal, a slight delay, or a change when the battery is cold or full can be normal. A spongy pedal, a pedal that drops, or a warning light is not normal."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Condition","What happens to regeneration"],"rows":[["Battery full","Regeneration is limited, because the battery cannot accept more energy"],["Battery very cold","Regeneration is limited until the battery warms up"],["Very low speed","The motor cannot slow the car, so friction brakes finish the stop"],["Hard braking or an ABS event","The friction brakes and the ABS take over"],["System fault","Regeneration may be reduced or off, with a warning"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"When you road-test an electrified vehicle after brake work, check the normal pedal feel at low speed, a firm stop and the behaviour when you lift off. After replacing pads, discs or hydraulic parts, follow the maker's service mode and bleeding procedure."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"If you notice smoke, a hissing sound, a sweet or solvent smell or heat from a battery, move away at once, warn others and call the emergency services. Do not try to put out a battery fire with a small extinguisher."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"When a vehicle comes in after an accident, check the underside for damage to the battery casing before you move it or connect anything."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m13-l6", "title": "Battery packs, the BMS and battery health", "minutes": 6, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The traction battery is the most expensive part of an electrified vehicle. To advise a customer, or to judge a used vehicle, you need to understand how a pack is built, how its capacity is worked out and what controls and wears it out. This lesson explains these things. Opening a pack is for trained specialists only."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Cells, modules and packs"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A lithium-ion **cell** gives about 3.2 to 3.7 V. Cells come in three shapes: **cylindrical** (like a large AA battery), **prismatic** (flat metal cases) and **pouch** (flat foil bags). Cells are connected in **series** to raise the voltage, and in **parallel** to raise the capacity. Groups of cells are packed into **modules**, and modules into a **pack** with cooling, wiring and the battery management system."},
      {"t":"example","title":"How big is the pack?","steps":["A pack has 96 cells in series, and three sets of such strings in parallel. This is written 96s3p. Each cell has a nominal voltage of 3.7 V and a capacity of 50 Ah.","Pack voltage = 96 × 3.7 = 355 V (nominal).","Pack capacity = 3 × 50 = 150 Ah.","Energy = 355 × 150 = 53,250 Wh, or about 53 kWh.","At 16 kWh per 100 km, the range is about 53 ÷ 16 × 100 = 330 km on a full charge. In practice the usable energy is a little less, and the real range depends on speed, temperature, load and driving style."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The battery management system"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **BMS** is the pack's brain. It measures the voltage of every cell group, the pack current and temperatures at many points. It calculates the state of charge and state of health, limits the charge and discharge power, balances the cells, controls the cooling and heating, closes and opens the contactors and monitors insulation."},
      {"t":"table","head":["BMS function","Why it matters"],"rows":[["Cell voltage monitoring","Over-charge or deep discharge of one cell can cause damage or fire"],["Temperature monitoring","Cells age quickly when hot and cannot take fast charge when cold"],["Cell balancing","Cells drift apart in charge. Balancing makes the weakest cell not limit the whole pack."],["State of charge and health","Gives the driver the range and tells the technician the condition"],["Power limits","Reduces power when cold, hot, nearly full or nearly empty"],["Insulation monitoring","Detects leakage between the high-voltage system and the body"],["Contactor control","Disconnects the pack if a fault or crash is detected"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Why batteries age"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Time and temperature:** a battery loses capacity as it ages, even if unused. High temperature speeds it up.","**Charge cycles:** each full cycle wears the cells a little. Shallow cycles are gentler.","**Charging habits:** repeated fast charging, charging to 100% and leaving it, or discharging to empty all increase wear.","**Cold weather:** reduces capacity and power temporarily, but does not usually damage the cells.","**Physical damage:** a hard impact, a puncture or water can destroy cells."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["State of health","What it means","What to advise"],"rows":[["Above about 90%","Very good","Normal use"],["About 80 to 90%","Good, range somewhat reduced","Normal use. Many warranties cover to 70%."],["About 70 to 80%","Noticeably reduced range","Check against the warranty. Plan for the use of the vehicle."],["Below about 70%","Poor","May qualify for a warranty repair. Needs a specialist assessment."]],"caption":"Figures are only a guide. The maker's warranty terms apply."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Checking a used vehicle's battery"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Ask for the service history and any battery tests carried out by the maker.","Read state of health and cell balance with a suitable scan tool. A large difference between the best and weakest cell group suggests a problem.","Check for warning lights, the charge port, and any signs of damage on the pack's casing.","Charge and drive the vehicle, and compare the range shown with what is expected.","Report your findings in writing. Advice about battery health is based on the data, and a customer must know that it is an estimate."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Second life and recycling"},
      {"t":"p","text":"When a battery is no longer good enough for a vehicle, it often still holds 70% or so of its capacity. It may be reused to store energy at a house or a business, or its metals are recovered by recycling. A damaged or old battery must go to an approved recycler, with the correct transport rules for hazardous goods. Never throw cells in ordinary waste."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Diagnostic examples"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 1: Will not go READY.** A hybrid shows a warning and will not go to READY. The 12 V battery reads 11.4 V at rest, and a load test shows it is weak. With a new 12 V battery the system goes READY. The weak 12 V battery could not operate the contactors and the control units reliably."},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 2: EV charges slowly at a public point.** An electric car takes 4 kW from a 22 kW public AC point. The on-board charger is rated at 11 kW, and the same car charges at 11 kW from another point. The first charge point is giving only 6 A because it is shared among several vehicles. The vehicle is working as designed."},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 3: Isolation fault after rain.** An EV sets an isolation fault after driving in heavy rain, and clears when dry. A scan tool shows low insulation resistance when wet. The vehicle is referred to a high-voltage technician, who finds water in a connector under the car and repairs it. A workshop without the training did the right thing by not trying to open the cable."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Never judge a high-voltage battery by looking or by measuring it directly. Use the scan tool's data and the maker's tests, and refer pack work to specialists."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"A damaged or swollen pack, a sweet or solvent smell, smoke or a hissing sound means danger. Move away, warn others and call the emergency services."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m13-l7", "title": "Charging modes, connectors and charging times", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A technician is often asked why a vehicle charges slowly, or why a cable does not fit. The answers lie in a few simple rules: how much power the supply gives, how the car and charger talk to each other, and which plug fits which socket. This lesson explains charging in plain terms, with worked calculations."},
      {"t":"h","text":"AC and DC charging"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The supply at a home is **alternating current**, but a battery needs **direct current**. In **AC charging**, the car's own **on-board charger** does the conversion, so the power is limited by that unit, often 3.7 to 22 kW. In **DC fast charging**, a large charger outside the car does the conversion and feeds the battery directly, so power can be 50 kW to 350 kW or more. The car's BMS controls how much power it will accept."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Mode","What it is","Typical power"],"rows":[["Mode 1","Plain cable to a socket. Not recommended for cars.","Up to about 3.7 kW"],["Mode 2","A portable cable with a control and protection box in the cable, plugged into a household socket","About 2.3 to 3.7 kW"],["Mode 3","A fixed wall box or public charge point with a control signal and protection","3.7 to 22 kW"],["Mode 4","DC fast charging from a large external charger","50 to 350 kW"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"How power is worked out"},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Single-phase AC power = voltage × current","caption":"For three-phase, multiply by √3 (1.73) times the line voltage and the current"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Home and public AC charging power","steps":["A single-phase supply of 230 V at 32 A gives 230 × 32 = 7,360 W, or 7.4 kW.","A single-phase supply at 16 A gives 230 × 16 = 3.7 kW.","A three-phase supply at 400 V and 16 A gives 1.73 × 400 × 16 = 11 kW. At 32 A it gives 22 kW.","The on-board charger must also support the power. A car with a 7.4 kW on-board charger will only take 7.4 kW from a 22 kW point."]},
      {"t":"example","title":"How long to charge?","steps":["A car needs 40 kWh to go from 20% to 95%. It charges at 7.4 kW from a wall box, and the charging efficiency is 90%.","Energy from the supply = 40 ÷ 0.90 = 44.4 kWh.","Time = 44.4 ÷ 7.4 = 6.0 hours.","On a 100 kW DC fast charger, charging 10% to 80% of a 53 kWh battery means adding 0.7 × 53 = 37 kWh. The charge rate falls as the battery fills, so the average is about 70 kW, and the time is 37 ÷ 70 = 0.53 hours, about 32 minutes."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Plugs and sockets"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Connector","Type","Where you meet it"],"rows":[["Type 1 (J1772)","AC, single-phase","North America, Japan, some imports"],["Type 2 (Mennekes)","AC, single or three-phase","Europe and many other regions"],["CCS1 and CCS2","Type 1 or 2 plus two DC pins","AC and DC in one socket"],["CHAdeMO","DC","Older Japanese vehicles"],["GB/T","AC and DC","China"],["NACS (Tesla)","AC and DC in one small plug","North America and some makers"]],"caption":"Adaptors exist, but a charge point must still give the right power and signals. Used imports from one region may need a different cable or adaptor."},
      {"t":"h","text":"How the car and charger talk"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In AC charging, a **control pilot** wire sends a small signal between the car and the charger. The charger sends a signal whose pulse width tells the car the highest current it may use. The car answers with a resistor that tells the charger it is ready. A **proximity pilot** wire tells the car that the plug is in, and what current the cable can carry. Only when both sides agree does the charger close its relay. In DC charging, the car and charger exchange digital messages, such as the battery's voltage and the current it asks for."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Protecting the home supply"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["A charging point at home must be installed by a competent electrician, with its own circuit, proper protection and earthing.","A charger runs for hours at high current, much more than most household loads. Never use an ordinary extension lead, a multi-plug adaptor or a lightweight plug. They overheat and cause fires.","The supply should have a residual current device (RCD) of the right type for DC leakage, as the charger's instructions require.","Check the cable, plug and socket for heat, discolouration and damage. A warm plug is a warning.","Use the charging schedule and limit settings to avoid charging at the peak time or to 100% when it is not needed."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Vehicle to load and vehicle to grid"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Some vehicles can give power as well as take it. **Vehicle-to-load** (V2L) supplies a socket on the car or an adaptor, to run tools, lights or a home during a power cut. **Vehicle-to-grid** (V2G) sends power back to the supply. These use the car's inverter and need correct equipment. Only suitable, approved devices should be connected to the vehicle, and only by qualified electricians."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Charging speed is limited by three things: the supply, the cable and charger, and the car. The slowest of them sets the speed."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never use a damaged cable, never charge in a flood or in heavy rain without the charge point being rated for it, and never touch the pins of a plug or socket."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m13-l8", "title": "Regenerative braking and drive systems", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Electrified vehicles recover energy that would be lost as heat when braking. This lesson explains how, and how the drive systems differ from conventional ones."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Regenerative braking"},
      {"t":"p","text":"When you lift off the accelerator or press the brake pedal, the motor acts as a **generator**. It turns the vehicle's motion into electricity, which charges the battery, and this slows the vehicle. This is called **regenerative braking**. It saves energy and extends the range."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Brake blending"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Regenerative braking can not stop the vehicle on its own in every situation, for example at a very low speed, when the battery is full or when the brake needs to be strong. The vehicle blends regeneration with the **friction brakes** (discs and pads) automatically, with the computer deciding how much of each to use. Many electrified vehicles use an electrically operated brake booster and a brake-by-wire system, so the pedal feel is controlled by software."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Transmissions and motors"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Most electric vehicles use a **single-speed reduction gear**, because an electric motor gives useful torque over a wide speed range. Hybrids may use a power-split device, a conventional automatic or a dual-clutch gearbox. Motor types in outline include permanent-magnet synchronous motors and induction motors."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Brake wear and service"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Because regeneration does much of the braking, pads and discs often wear slowly. But they still need service. Used rarely, discs can rust, calipers can seize and pads can glaze, since the brakes are not used hard enough to clean them. Brake fluid still absorbs water, and still needs to be changed on time. Brake service on some vehicles needs a scan tool to put the system in a service mode."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Item","On electrified vehicles"],"rows":[["Brake pads","Often last longer, but check for rust and sticking"],["Brake fluid","Still absorbs water. Renew at the interval"],["Tyres","Heavier vehicles and high torque can increase tyre wear"],["Transmission fluid","Reduction gearbox oil still needs a change at the interval"]],"caption":"Wear items"},
      {"t":"example","title":"How much energy does regeneration recover?","steps":["A car of 1,600 kg is travelling at 72 km/h, which is 20 m/s. Its kinetic energy is ½ × 1,600 × 20² = 320,000 J.","One kilowatt-hour is 3,600,000 J, so this energy is 320,000 ÷ 3,600,000 = 0.089 kWh, or 89 Wh.","If the system recovers 65% of it, 0.058 kWh (58 Wh) goes back to the battery.","This is a small amount for each stop, but over a day of city driving it adds up, and it saves the brake pads from most of their work."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"The brake system is a safety system. Whatever the vehicle type, test the brakes after any work, and follow the maker's procedure for bleeding and service mode."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Advise owners of electrified vehicles to use the brakes firmly now and then, so that the discs and pads are cleaned."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m13-l9", "title": "How hybrid systems operate", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A hybrid vehicle uses an engine and a motor together, controlled by a computer that decides at every moment which should work. This lesson explains the common layouts, how a full hybrid behaves in real driving and how that affects the technician. The aim is to understand the system, so that you know what is normal and when to ask for help."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Hybrid layouts"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Layout","How it works","Typical example"],"rows":[["Series","The engine only drives a generator. The motor drives the wheels.","Range-extender vehicles"],["Parallel","The engine and the motor both drive the wheels through a gearbox","Many mild and full hybrids"],["Series-parallel (power-split)","A planetary gear set joins the engine, a generator and a motor. The system can act as series or parallel.","Many full hybrids"],["P0 (belt starter-generator)","A motor-generator on the engine belt, usually on 48 V","Mild hybrids"],["P2 (between engine and gearbox)","The motor sits next to a clutch behind the engine","Parallel hybrids and many plug-in hybrids"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The power-split system"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A power-split hybrid has a **planetary gear set**. The engine drives the planet carrier, the generator (MG1) is on the sun gear, and the main motor (MG2) and the output are on the ring gear. By changing the speed of the generator, the computer changes the gear ratio smoothly, so there are no gear changes. The engine can run at its most efficient speed while the wheels turn at any speed. The generator also starts the engine. It is a clever system with few wearing parts, but its behaviour is very different from a normal car."},
      {"t":"h","text":"A typical drive"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["**Start:** in READY mode, the engine is off. The motor moves the car silently, using the battery.","**Pulling away and light cruising:** the car stays on electric power for as long as the battery and the demand allow.","**Harder acceleration:** the engine starts, and both help. The engine may be started by the generator in a fraction of a second.","**Steady speed:** the engine runs at its best efficiency, and part of its output drives the generator to charge the battery.","**Braking and coasting:** the motor acts as a generator, and the engine usually stops.","**Stop:** the engine stops. The 12 V systems, air conditioning and others may need the engine to run sometimes."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"The engine often starts without warning, even with the car stationary, to charge the battery, warm up the catalytic converter or run the air conditioning. A hybrid in READY that sounds silent is not safe to approach."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The state-of-charge window"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A full hybrid does not use the whole battery. The computer keeps the state of charge within a narrow band, such as 40 to 60%, which is gentle on the cells and leaves room to accept energy from braking. The battery is small, only 1 to 2 kWh, but it is cycled many times. A plug-in hybrid uses a wider window, for example 80 to 20%, to give electric range, then works like a full hybrid."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Mild hybrids"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A mild hybrid with a 48 V system uses a belt-driven starter-generator to start the engine smoothly, to help it a little during acceleration and to recover energy. It cannot drive the car alone. A 48 V system is below the 60 V that is called high voltage, but it can still deliver large currents and cause burns and fires. Use the manual and take the same care that you would with a 12 V battery."},
      {"t":"h","text":"What is different for the technician"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Always check whether the vehicle is **READY** before you work on it, and switch it off fully, with the key away from the car.","An engine that does not run is not a fault. A hybrid engine runs only some of the time. Watch for the engine starting.","The engine oil, spark plugs and coolant are changed on a time basis as well as a distance basis, since the engine runs less.","Brake wear is lower and corrosion higher. Check the brakes carefully.","Diagnose with the scan tool. Look at battery state of charge, cell group voltages, temperature and contactor status.","Use only the specified oil, coolant and fluids. Some have special requirements, such as insulation properties for the transmission fluid in the electric parts."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A silent hybrid is not an off hybrid. Treat it as running until you have proved it is off."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"When a hybrid is brought in with a complaint about fuel use, ask how it is used. Short trips in the cold, a lot of air conditioning and high speed all reduce the benefit."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m13-l10", "title": "Service and diagnosis of electrified vehicles", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Most workshops will see hybrid and electric vehicles, and every technician needs to know what they may do and when to ask for help."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Scope of work for an ordinary workshop"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Tasks that do not involve high-voltage systems are normally allowed for trained mechanics, with care. These include tyre changes and tyre pressure checks, wheel alignment, suspension and steering work, brake pad and disc service (if the system's procedure is followed), cabin filters, wiper blades, lamps and 12 V battery tests and replacement. Even for these, switch the vehicle off, remove the key from the vehicle, and note any high-voltage warnings."},
      {"t":"h","text":"When to refer to a specialist"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Any work on or near orange cables, the high-voltage battery, the inverter, the motor or the charger.","Any fault with a high-voltage warning, isolation fault code or a charging fault.","Any vehicle that has been in an accident or flooded.","Air conditioning compressors on electrified vehicles, which are driven by high voltage and use a special oil."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Lifting, towing and accidents"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Lift the vehicle only at the maker's lift points, because the battery under the floor can be crushed. Most electric vehicles should be towed on a **flatbed**, because towing with the drive wheels on the road can generate electricity in the motor and damage it. Follow the manual. After a crash, do not touch orange cables. Switch off the vehicle, remove the key, and keep others away. For fire, call the emergency services, and tell them it is an electric or hybrid vehicle."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Warning lights and codes"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Electrified vehicles show warning lamps for the high-voltage system, such as a red vehicle-with-lightning symbol. Codes about **isolation faults**, **battery temperature** or **contactor faults** need a specialist. A scan tool can read the codes, but not all workshops are allowed to clear them or perform battery tests."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The 12 V battery check"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Switch off the vehicle and wait for the systems to shut down.","Find the 12 V battery. It may be in the boot, under a seat or in the engine bay. Check the manual.","Inspect the battery and terminals and clean any corrosion.","Measure the resting voltage. A healthy battery reads about 12.6 V.","Test it with a conductance tester, and charge it with a charger suitable for the type of battery if it is low. Do not use the high-voltage system to jump start."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Routine care of an electric vehicle"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Item","Notes"],"rows":[["Brakes","Pads and discs last longer, but can corrode and stick if the friction brakes are rarely used. Clean, lubricate and check them at each service."],["Tyres","Weight and instant torque raise wear. Check pressures and tread more often. Use tyres with the correct load rating."],["Coolant","Separate cooling circuits for the battery, motor and electronics. Use the maker's coolant and procedure. Many have long service intervals."],["Reduction gear oil","Check and change at the maker's interval. Use the oil specified."],["Cabin filter and air conditioning","The A/C compressor is electric, with special oil. Refrigerant work has the same rules as any vehicle, plus high-voltage precautions."],["12 V battery","Test it regularly. It causes many faults."],["Software","Check for updates. Many faults are fixed by software."]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Do not use a battery charger or jump-starting pack on the high-voltage battery. Only connect to the 12 V battery or the points the manual shows."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Knowing your limit is a professional skill. If in doubt, stop, and ask a trained specialist."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m13-l11", "title": "Accidents, recovery and storage of electrified vehicles", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Electrified vehicles need extra care after a crash and when they are stored or recovered. This lesson explains the points that every workshop should know: how to recognise a damaged vehicle, how to move it, what to do about a fire risk and how to store it. It is about keeping people safe, not about repairing high-voltage parts."},
      {"t":"h","text":"After a crash"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Treat any collision damage to an electrified vehicle as a possible high-voltage hazard, even a minor one. A damaged cable or battery casing may not be visible.","The vehicle is designed to shut down the high-voltage system if a crash is sensed, but this does not always happen. Never assume it has.","Look at the underside for damage to the battery, the casing, orange cables and cooling pipes. Do not touch them.","Look for leaks, smoke, noises like hissing or popping, a smell, or unusual heat from the battery.","Keep people away. Do not charge or jump-start the vehicle.","Tell the owner, the insurer and the recovery driver that it is an electrified vehicle. Use the manufacturer's **emergency response guide** or **rescue sheet**, which shows the location of the battery, cables and cut points."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Recovery and towing"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Situation","Correct action"],"rows":[["Electric or hybrid, not able to drive","Use a flatbed truck. Do not tow with the drive wheels on the ground unless the maker's manual allows it, because the motor can generate electricity and the transmission can be damaged."],["Electric vehicle with a flat 12 V battery and a locked parking brake","Some have a release procedure, or need a 12 V jump to unlock. Use the manual."],["Wheels that cannot be freed","Use skates or dollies under the drive wheels if the maker allows."],["Vehicle with damaged battery","Recovery by a trained operator, away from people and buildings. Tell them about the damage."]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"A damaged battery in the workshop"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Do not bring it into the workshop. Park it outside, in an open place, well away from buildings and other vehicles. A common advice is a distance of at least 10 to 15 metres.","Put up barriers and a sign. Tell all staff.","Inform the manufacturer's emergency line or the specialist who will remove it.","Monitor it. A damaged battery can start a fire hours or even days later. Some insurers and makers require a quarantine period.","If it begins to smoke, hiss or smell, move away, call the fire service and tell them it is an electric vehicle. Do not use a small extinguisher on a battery fire. Large amounts of water are used by trained crews."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fire and flood"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["A battery fire burns very hot, produces toxic fumes and may reignite. Leave it to trained fire fighters.","A vehicle that has been in floodwater, especially salt water, is a risk. Salt water causes corrosion and short circuits inside the pack that appear days later. Move it away from other vehicles and buildings and tell the owner and the insurer.","Do not charge a vehicle that has been flooded or that shows damage at the charge port."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Storing an electrified vehicle"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Item","Advice"],"rows":[["State of charge","Store at about 30 to 60%, as the maker advises. Not full and not empty."],["12 V battery","Keep it charged. A flat 12 V battery can leave the vehicle unable to wake up or charge. Use a maintainer."],["Place","Cool, dry and ventilated, away from direct heat and from flammable materials"],["Charging","Check the charge every month or two, and top up. Some vehicles discharge slowly even when off."],["Long storage","Follow the maker's procedure, which may include disconnecting the 12 V battery or using a special mode."]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"If an electrified vehicle has been in a crash or flood, treat it as a hazard until a trained person has checked it."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never cut, pierce or touch orange cables on a crashed vehicle, never charge a damaged vehicle, and never move a vehicle with a damaged battery into a workshop."}
    ]}
  ],
  "practical": {"title":"Hybrid and EV recognition and safety awareness","objective":"Identify the type and main components of a hybrid or electric vehicle, find the warning labels and orange cables without touching high-voltage parts, and carry out the 12 V battery check.","time":"60 minutes","tools":["A hybrid or electric vehicle (or a training display) with the manual","Digital multimeter and a conductance battery tester","Safety glasses, gloves and wheel chocks","A checklist of components and warning labels","Notepad"],"safety":["Do not touch, open, unplug or cut any orange cable or high-voltage component.","Make sure the vehicle is switched off, not in READY and the key is away from the cabin.","Do not work alone, and do not work on a wet vehicle or in wet conditions.","Do not use any tool across the high-voltage terminals.","Stop and call the instructor if you see damage, smoke, leaks or a warning light."],"steps":["Find the type of vehicle from the badges, the manual and the VIN and write it on your sheet.","Check that the vehicle is switched off, not in READY and the key is away from the cabin.","Chock the wheels and open the bonnet. Locate the 12 V battery and the orange cables you can see.","Without touching them, list every high-voltage component and warning label that you can see.","Use the manual layout diagram to mark the position of the battery, inverter, charger and service disconnect.","Note the charge port type and the connector, if the vehicle has one.","Find the 12 V battery and inspect the terminals.","Measure the 12 V battery's resting voltage and record it.","Test the 12 V battery with the conductance tester and record the result.","Clean the terminals if there is any corrosion and tighten them.","Write a short note on what work an ordinary workshop may do on this vehicle and what must be referred.","Close the bonnet and leave the vehicle as you found it."],"checks":["I identified the vehicle type and its main high-voltage components.","I found the warning labels and orange cables without touching them.","I measured and tested the 12 V battery correctly.","I explained what I may do and what I must refer to a specialist.","I followed the safety rules and stayed away from the high-voltage parts."]},
  "quiz": [
    {"id":"m13-q01","q":"What colour are high-voltage cables on a hybrid or electric vehicle?","options":["Yellow","Green","Red","Orange"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m13-l1","explain":"Orange cables mean high voltage. Do not touch, cut or unplug them unless you are trained and authorised."},
    {"id":"m13-q02","q":"Which type of vehicle has a battery and motor only, with no engine or fuel tank?","options":["A mild hybrid vehicle with a small motor","A full hybrid vehicle with an engine","A battery electric vehicle","A plug-in hybrid vehicle with an engine"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l1","explain":"A BEV runs only on electricity from its traction battery."},
    {"id":"m13-q03","q":"What does a mild hybrid usually do?","options":["Uses a small motor-generator, often 48 V, to assist the engine and recover energy","Drives the vehicle on electricity alone for a long distance without any engine at all","Uses a large battery that is charged from the mains supply at home","Uses a fuel cell to make electricity from hydrogen gas in a tank"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m13-l1","explain":"A mild hybrid can not drive on its motor alone."},
    {"id":"m13-q04","q":"What does the READY light on a hybrid mean?","options":["The 12 V battery needs to be charged straight away to avoid damage","The high-voltage battery has failed and needs a service at the dealer","The vehicle is switched off and it is completely safe to work on it","The vehicle is on and can move, and the engine may start without warning"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m13-l1","explain":"Always make sure the vehicle is off and not READY before working near it."},
    {"id":"m13-q05","q":"Which unit measures the energy a battery stores?","options":["The kilowatt, a unit of power","The ampere, a unit of current","The ohm, a unit of resistance","The kilowatt-hour"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m13-l1","explain":"Energy is in kWh. A kilowatt is a rate of energy use, a unit of power."},
    {"id":"m13-q06","q":"What does the inverter do?","options":["It cools the traction battery by using the vehicle's refrigerant system","It charges the 12 V battery directly from the mains supply at the wall socket","It changes DC from the battery to AC for the motor and controls the motor","It stores the energy that is used for driving the wheels of the vehicle"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l2","explain":"The inverter also lets the motor act as a generator when braking."},
    {"id":"m13-q07","q":"What does the DC-DC converter do?","options":["It raises the 12 V of the small battery so that it can power the electric motor","It lowers the high voltage to 12 V to charge the 12 V battery and run the vehicle systems","It controls the speed of the motor from the position of the accelerator pedal","It converts mains AC into DC to charge the traction battery at the charge port"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l2","explain":"It does the job of the alternator in a conventional vehicle."},
    {"id":"m13-q08","q":"Why is the 12 V battery important on an electric vehicle?","options":["It runs the control modules and the contactors, so a flat one can stop the vehicle starting","It is used only for the radio and the clock when the vehicle is parked","It is the only battery that stores the energy for driving the vehicle","It powers the electric motor at every speed and when the vehicle accelerates hard"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m13-l2","explain":"Many faults on electrified vehicles come from a weak 12 V battery."},
    {"id":"m13-q09","q":"What is the job of the battery management system?","options":["It makes the electric motor turn faster whenever the driver presses the pedal","It charges the 12 V battery from the alternator on the engine","It monitors cell voltage, temperature and charge, and switches the battery on and off","It controls the air conditioning compressor and the cabin heater only"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l2","explain":"The BMS protects the battery and decides when the contactors may close."},
    {"id":"m13-q10","q":"What is the purpose of the pre-charge contactor?","options":["To cool the battery before charging","To lock the charging cable in the port","It fills the capacitors slowly","To measure the cell voltages"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l2","explain":"A resistor path charges the inverter's capacitors gently. The main contactors close only when the voltage is nearly equal, so there is no big surge."},
    {"id":"m13-q11","q":"What do the stator windings of a motor do in an electric car?","options":["They make the rotating field","They store the energy for the next journey","They cool the battery pack during charging","They measure the vehicle speed on the road"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m13-l3","explain":"The inverter feeds three-phase currents to the stator, making a rotating magnetic field that drags the rotor round."},
    {"id":"m13-q12","q":"A motor gives 250 N·m up to 4,000 rpm. What is its power at 4,000 rpm?","options":["About 1,000 kW","About 105 kW","About 10.5 kW","About 250 kW"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m13-l3","explain":"Power = 250 × 4,000 ÷ 9,549 = 104.7 kW. Above base speed, power stays constant and torque falls."},
    {"id":"m13-q13","q":"A motor with a 9:1 reduction gearbox turns at 8,000 rpm. How fast do the wheels turn?","options":["About 72,000 rpm","About 8,000 rpm","About 889 rpm","About 9 rpm"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m13-l3","explain":"The reduction divides the speed by 9: 8,000 ÷ 9 = 889 rpm. The torque is multiplied by 9."},
    {"id":"m13-q14","q":"Why do inverters need their own cooling circuit?","options":["The inverter stores the vehicle's coolant","Switching makes heat that cuts power","The inverter freezes in cold weather","The coolant produces the inverter signal"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l3","explain":"Switching losses make heat in the power semiconductors. Overheating reduces power or damages the parts."},
    {"id":"m13-q15","q":"What does a resolver on a motor shaft tell the inverter?","options":["The exact position of the rotor","The state of charge of the battery","The temperature of the coolant","The speed limit on the road"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l3","explain":"The inverter must time its currents to the rotor position. A failed resolver stops or judders the motor."},
    {"id":"m13-q16","q":"Who is allowed to work on high-voltage systems?","options":["Only people who are trained, certified and authorised","Anyone who has a pair of rubber gloves and a few years of workshop experience","Any mechanic who has worked on petrol and diesel engines for many years","Anyone who has switched off the ignition and removed the key"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m13-l4","explain":"High-voltage work needs specific training and authority."},
    {"id":"m13-q17","q":"What is the purpose of the service disconnect?","options":["To lock all of the doors while the vehicle is being serviced in the workshop","To disconnect the 12 V battery from the starter motor when it is cold","To reset the service reminder after the oil change has been done","To break the high-voltage battery circuit so that the system can be isolated"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l4","explain":"It is one of the steps of an isolation procedure."},
    {"id":"m13-q18","q":"Why must you wait after switching off a high-voltage vehicle before working on it?","options":["The engine needs time to stop running after the vehicle is switched off","Stored energy in capacitors takes time to fall to a safe level","The tyres need time to settle after the vehicle has been parked","The brakes need time to cool down after the last journey of the day"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m13-l4","explain":"The manufacturer states the wait, often several minutes."},
    {"id":"m13-q19","q":"What must be done after the wait and before touching a high-voltage part?","options":["Verify with a suitable meter that no dangerous voltage remains","Assume that it is safe, because the wait time has now been completed","Touch it briefly with a dry hand to check whether it is live","Ask the owner if the vehicle has been switched off at the dashboard"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l4","explain":"Never assume. Prove it is dead before you work."},
    {"id":"m13-q20","q":"What should you do before using insulating gloves?","options":["Inspect and air-test them for holes and damage","Apply oil so that they stay flexible when they are worn on the job","Wash them in hot water to soften them and make them more flexible","Wear them over a pair of metal rings so that the gloves fit well"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m13-l4","explain":"A small hole or crack in an insulating glove can allow a lethal shock, so every pair must be inspected and air-tested before each use."},
    {"id":"m13-q21","q":"A person is being shocked by a high-voltage cable. What is the first safe action?","options":["Wait for the cable to stop sparking before going near the person","Pour water over the cable to cool it and stop the current","Do not touch them; switch off the supply or use an insulated hook to pull them clear","Grab their arm firmly and pull them away from the cable at once"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m13-l4","explain":"Touching them would make you a casualty too. Then call for help."},
    {"id":"m13-q22","q":"Why should two trained people be present for high-voltage work?","options":["Two people work faster than one person, so the job will be done sooner","The law requires two keys for the vehicle to be switched off","The second person can hold the tools while the first one works","One can act in an emergency, such as rescue and calling for help"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l4","explain":"Never work alone on a high-voltage vehicle."},
    {"id":"m13-q23","q":"What is the aim of awareness-level training in high-voltage safety?","options":["To recognise the hazard and keep clear","To allow work on the battery pack","To teach how to cut orange cables","To authorise work on charging equipment"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l4","explain":"Awareness is for everyone in the workshop. It does not authorise any work on high-voltage parts."},
    {"id":"m13-q24","q":"Which cell chemistry is most common in newer electric vehicles?","options":["Lead-acid","Nickel-iron","Lithium-ion","Zinc-carbon"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l5","explain":"Different lithium-ion types vary in energy density and cost."},
    {"id":"m13-q25","q":"What is state of health (SoH) of a battery?","options":["The voltage of the small 12 V battery at the front of the vehicle","The temperature of the battery cells as measured by the sensors","How much capacity remains compared to when it was new","How full the battery is at a given moment, as a percentage"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l5","explain":"A scan tool or the vehicle display can show it."},
    {"id":"m13-q26","q":"What is thermal runaway?","options":["A cooling fan that keeps running for too long after the vehicle is switched off","A chain reaction in which a damaged cell overheats and spreads to others","A motor that turns at a higher speed than normal on a long hill","A battery that charges too slowly when it is connected to a wallbox"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m13-l5","explain":"It can cause toxic gas, flames and re-ignition later."},
    {"id":"m13-q27","q":"What should you do with a vehicle that has a crash-damaged battery?","options":["Drive it to the nearest workshop slowly, with the hazard lamps on","Leave it indoors with the other vehicles to keep it dry and safe","Charge it to see if it still works, and then drive it to the workshop","Park it outdoors away from buildings and other vehicles and warn the owner"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l5","explain":"A damaged battery is a fire risk and may re-ignite."},
    {"id":"m13-q28","q":"Which charging type is the slowest?","options":["A rapid public charger","Level 1 from a household socket","Level 2 AC wallbox","DC fast charging"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m13-l5","explain":"It suits overnight charging of small batteries."},
    {"id":"m13-q29","q":"A pack is 96s3p with 3.7 V and 50 Ah cells. What is its energy?","options":["About 17.8 kWh","About 355 kWh","About 5.3 kWh","About 53 kWh"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l6","explain":"Voltage 96 × 3.7 = 355 V and capacity 3 × 50 = 150 Ah. Energy = 355 × 150 = 53,250 Wh, or 53 kWh."},
    {"id":"m13-q30","q":"What does cell balancing do?","options":["It makes all the cells run much hotter","It stops weak cells limiting the pack","It raises the pack voltage a great deal","It measures the insulation of the wiring"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l6","explain":"Cells drift apart in charge. Balancing brings them back together so the whole pack can be used."},
    {"id":"m13-q31","q":"Which habit is gentle on the battery?","options":["Avoid long periods at 100% or 0%","Fast charge at every single stop","Leave it fully empty for a month","Charge it in the full midday sun"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m13-l6","explain":"Extreme charge levels and heat both speed up ageing. Moderate levels and cool temperatures are better."},
    {"id":"m13-q32","q":"A used EV shows state of health 74%. What should you tell the buyer?","options":["The battery is practically brand new","The whole battery must be thrown away","There is nothing at all wrong with it","Range is reduced; check the warranty"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m13-l6","explain":"74% means about a quarter of the capacity is lost. Check the maker's warranty and plan range accordingly."},
    {"id":"m13-q33","q":"A single-phase supply is 230 V at 32 A. What is the charging power?","options":["About 3.7 kW","About 11 kW","About 7.4 kW","About 22 kW"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l7","explain":"Power = 230 × 32 = 7,360 W = 7.4 kW. A three-phase supply at 400 V and 32 A gives 22 kW."},
    {"id":"m13-q34","q":"Which limits the charging power of a car with a 7.4 kW on-board charger on a 22 kW AC point?","options":["The charge point, at 22 kW","The building supply, at 100 kW","The length of the cable only","The car's on-board charger"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m13-l7","explain":"The slowest part sets the speed. The car's charger converts the AC, and it cannot accept more than its rating."},
    {"id":"m13-q35","q":"It takes 44.4 kWh from the supply and the charger gives 7.4 kW. How long does it take?","options":["About 0.17 hours","About 60 hours","About 6 hours","About 328 hours"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l7","explain":"Time = energy ÷ power = 44.4 ÷ 7.4 = 6.0 hours."},
    {"id":"m13-q36","q":"Why should an ordinary extension lead not be used to charge a car?","options":["It can overheat on a long, high load","It makes the car charge much faster","It changes the battery chemistry slowly","It lets the charge port rust through"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m13-l7","explain":"A car draws 10 to 32 A for hours. A thin extension lead or adaptor overheats and can start a fire."},
    {"id":"m13-q37","q":"What does the proximity pilot signal tell the car?","options":["The weather at the charge point now","Plug connected, and cable rating","The state of charge of the battery","The price of the electricity used"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l7","explain":"The proximity pilot shows the plug is in and tells the car what current the cable can carry safely."},
    {"id":"m13-q38","q":"How does regenerative braking slow a vehicle?","options":["The engine is switched off and the gearbox locks the wheels at once","The brake pads are pressed against the discs harder by the computer","The wheels are connected to the battery by a cable that slows them","The motor acts as a generator and turns motion into electricity"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l8","explain":"Regeneration recovers energy that would otherwise be lost as heat in the brakes, which saves energy and extends the driving range of the vehicle."},
    {"id":"m13-q39","q":"What is brake blending?","options":["Pressing both of the brake pedals at the same time with each foot","The computer combines regenerative braking with the friction brakes","Mixing two types of brake fluid in the master cylinder to improve feel","Changing the pads and the discs at the same time at a service"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m13-l8","explain":"The computer decides how much braking comes from regeneration and how much from the friction brakes, and the driver does not notice the change."},
    {"id":"m13-q40","q":"Why can brake discs on an electric vehicle rust or seize?","options":["The wheels turn much faster than on any other type of vehicle","The motor makes the discs very hot, so they warp when they cool","The friction brakes are used less, so they are not cleaned by use","The brake fluid is much thinner on electric vehicles than on others"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l8","explain":"Service them as the maker requires and advise the owner."},
    {"id":"m13-q41","q":"What type of transmission do most electric vehicles use?","options":["A single-speed reduction gear","A six-speed manual gearbox","A four-speed automatic gearbox","A continuously variable belt drive"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m13-l8","explain":"An electric motor gives useful torque across a wide speed range."},
    {"id":"m13-q42","q":"A 1,600 kg car at 72 km/h has about how much kinetic energy?","options":["About 3,200 J","About 32,000,000 J","About 20 J","About 320,000 J"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l8","explain":"Energy = ½ × 1,600 × 20² = 320,000 J, or 0.089 kWh. Regeneration recovers part of it."},
    {"id":"m13-q43","q":"In a power-split hybrid, what does the planetary gear set allow?","options":["Direct drive between the engine and wheels only","Use of a clutch pedal by the driver","Smooth ratio changing without gear shifts","Storage of fuel in the gear housing"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l9","explain":"By changing the generator speed, the computer changes the ratio continuously, without gear changes."},
    {"id":"m13-q44","q":"Why does a hybrid's engine sometimes start when the car is stationary?","options":["Because the key has been left in the lock","To charge the battery or run the A/C","Because the tyres are low","Because the brakes are on"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m13-l9","explain":"The engine runs when the system needs it for charging, heating or cooling. A silent hybrid is not necessarily off."},
    {"id":"m13-q45","q":"What is a 48 V mild hybrid system?","options":["A belt starter-generator","A full electric drive with no engine","A hydrogen fuel cell stack","A solar panel on the roof"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m13-l9","explain":"A mild hybrid helps the engine and recovers energy, but cannot move the car on electric power alone."},
    {"id":"m13-q46","q":"Which job may an ordinary trained mechanic normally do on an electric vehicle?","options":["Change tyres and test or replace the 12 V battery","Replace the inverter, because it is the same as a normal part","Open the casing of the high-voltage battery and replace a cell","Cut and splice the orange cables where they are damaged or worn"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m13-l10","explain":"Only high-voltage work needs specialist training."},
    {"id":"m13-q47","q":"Why should most electric vehicles be towed on a flatbed?","options":["Towing with the drive wheels on the road can damage the motor","Flatbeds are cheaper than ordinary tow trucks, so garages prefer them","The brakes do not work when the vehicle is towed on its wheels","The battery needs to be recharged on the way, so the flatbed does it"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l10","explain":"Towing with the drive wheels on the road can turn the motor and generate electricity or damage it, so follow the maker's towing instructions and use a flatbed."},
    {"id":"m13-q48","q":"What should you do after an accident involving an electric vehicle?","options":["Move the vehicle indoors straight away to keep it dry and safe","Switch off, remove the key, keep others away and do not touch orange cables","Cut the orange cables to isolate the battery before anyone gets near","Connect a charger to the battery to keep the systems switched on"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l10","explain":"Treat the vehicle as live and call for trained help."},
    {"id":"m13-q49","q":"A high-voltage warning light and an isolation fault code appear on a vehicle. What should you do?","options":["Clear the code and return the vehicle to the owner as it is","Unplug the orange connectors one by one to find the fault","Replace the 12 V battery and hope that the lamp goes out","Refer the vehicle to a trained high-voltage specialist"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m13-l10","explain":"Isolation faults can mean a dangerous leak from the high-voltage system to the body."},
    {"id":"m13-q50","q":"Where may you connect a jump starter on an electric vehicle?","options":["To the traction battery terminals under the floor of the vehicle","Only to the 12 V battery or the points shown in the manual","To the charging port, using a cable from a second vehicle","To the orange cables near the inverter, using the largest clamps"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l10","explain":"Never connect a charger or jump pack to the high-voltage system."},
    {"id":"m13-q51","q":"Why should the vehicle be lifted only at the maker's lift points?","options":["The tyres will leave marks on the floor of the workshop bay","The battery under the floor can be crushed or damaged by a wrong lift point","The vehicle may roll off the lift in neutral when it is raised","The suspension will stay compressed afterwards and the vehicle will sit low"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m13-l10","explain":"Damaging a battery casing can cause a fire."},
    {"id":"m13-q52","q":"How should a crashed electric vehicle with a damaged battery be moved?","options":["Towed with its drive wheels on the ground","A trained operator on a flatbed","Pushed into the workshop by hand","Charged and driven away"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m13-l11","explain":"Damaged vehicles need a flatbed and a trained operator, and they must not go into a workshop."},
    {"id":"m13-q53","q":"Where should a vehicle with a damaged high-voltage battery be parked?","options":["Inside the workshop beside the lift","In a closed garage to keep it dry","Next to the fuel store","Outside, well away from buildings"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l11","explain":"A damaged battery can ignite hours or days later. Keep it in the open, with space around it."},
    {"id":"m13-q54","q":"Why is a flooded electric vehicle a continuing risk?","options":["Salt water causes hidden corrosion","The water makes the battery stronger","The vehicle becomes lighter","Its tyres cannot be repaired"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m13-l11","explain":"Moisture in the pack and connectors corrodes parts. Faults and fires can appear days or weeks later."},
    {"id":"m13-q55","q":"At what charge is a vehicle usually stored for a long period?","options":["100%, fully charged and topped up","0%, with the battery fully empty","About 30 to 60%","Exactly 90% at all times of year"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m13-l11","explain":"Neither full nor empty is best. Follow the maker's advice and keep the 12 V battery charged."}
  ],
  "cards": [
    {"id":"m13-c01","front":"What colour are high-voltage cables?","back":"Orange.","lesson":"m13-l1"},
    {"id":"m13-c02","front":"What can a mild hybrid not do?","back":"Drive on its motor alone.","lesson":"m13-l1"},
    {"id":"m13-c03","front":"What is a plug-in hybrid?","back":"A hybrid with a larger battery that can be charged from the mains.","lesson":"m13-l1"},
    {"id":"m13-c04","front":"What does the READY light mean?","back":"The vehicle is on and can move. The engine may start without warning.","lesson":"m13-l1"},
    {"id":"m13-c05","front":"What is the difference between kW and kWh?","back":"kW is power (a rate). kWh is energy (an amount). 1 kWh is 1 kW for an hour.","lesson":"m13-l1"},
    {"id":"m13-c06","front":"What does the inverter do?","back":"It changes DC to AC for the motor and controls the motor.","lesson":"m13-l2"},
    {"id":"m13-c07","front":"What does the DC-DC converter do?","back":"It lowers high voltage to 12 V to charge the 12 V battery.","lesson":"m13-l2"},
    {"id":"m13-c08","front":"What does the BMS do?","back":"It monitors the battery cells and controls the contactors.","lesson":"m13-l2"},
    {"id":"m13-c09","front":"Why does the 12 V battery matter?","back":"It runs the modules and contactors. A flat one can stop the vehicle starting.","lesson":"m13-l2"},
    {"id":"m13-c10","front":"What does the pre-charge circuit do?","back":"It charges the inverter capacitors slowly through a resistor before the main contactors close.","lesson":"m13-l2"},
    {"id":"m13-c11","front":"Shape of an electric motor's torque curve?","back":"Constant torque up to the base speed. Above it, constant power and falling torque.","lesson":"m13-l3"},
    {"id":"m13-c12","front":"What is the job of the traction inverter?","back":"Changes battery DC into three-phase AC for the motor, and lets the motor charge the battery on braking.","lesson":"m13-l3"},
    {"id":"m13-c13","front":"What does a resolver do?","back":"It tells the inverter the exact position of the motor's rotor.","lesson":"m13-l3"},
    {"id":"m13-c14","front":"Who may work on high-voltage systems?","back":"Only trained, certified and authorised people.","lesson":"m13-l4"},
    {"id":"m13-c15","front":"What is a service disconnect?","back":"A plug or switch that breaks the high-voltage battery circuit.","lesson":"m13-l4"},
    {"id":"m13-c16","front":"What are the isolation steps in outline?","back":"Switch off, remove the key, remove the service disconnect, wait, verify dead.","lesson":"m13-l4"},
    {"id":"m13-c17","front":"What must you do before gloves are used?","back":"Inspect and air-test them.","lesson":"m13-l4"},
    {"id":"m13-c18","front":"What if someone is being shocked?","back":"Do not touch them. Switch off or use an insulated hook, then call for help.","lesson":"m13-l4"},
    {"id":"m13-c19","front":"What is state of charge?","back":"How full the battery is, in percent.","lesson":"m13-l5"},
    {"id":"m13-c20","front":"What is state of health?","back":"How much capacity the battery has left compared with new.","lesson":"m13-l5"},
    {"id":"m13-c21","front":"What is thermal runaway?","back":"A chain reaction in which an overheated cell causes others to overheat.","lesson":"m13-l5"},
    {"id":"m13-c22","front":"What do you do with a crash-damaged battery?","back":"Park it outdoors away from buildings, and warn the owner and the emergency services.","lesson":"m13-l5"},
    {"id":"m13-c23","front":"How do you work out pack energy?","back":"Cells in series × cell voltage × (cells in parallel × cell Ah) gives Wh. For example 96s3p of 3.7 V, 50 Ah is about 53 kWh.","lesson":"m13-l6"},
    {"id":"m13-c24","front":"What are the main BMS functions?","back":"Monitors cells, balances them, limits power, controls cooling and contactors and watches insulation.","lesson":"m13-l6"},
    {"id":"m13-c25","front":"Causes of battery ageing?","back":"Time, high temperature, many cycles, fast charging, leaving at 0% or 100%, and physical damage.","lesson":"m13-l6"},
    {"id":"m13-c26","front":"Charging power formulas?","back":"Single-phase: V × A (230 V × 32 A = 7.4 kW). Three-phase: 1.73 × V × A (400 V × 16 A = 11 kW).","lesson":"m13-l7"},
    {"id":"m13-c27","front":"What sets the charging speed?","back":"The slowest of the supply, the cable and charger, and the car's on-board charger or battery.","lesson":"m13-l7"},
    {"id":"m13-c28","front":"Charging modes 1 to 4?","back":"1 plain cable, 2 cable with a control box, 3 wall box or charge point, 4 DC fast charger.","lesson":"m13-l7"},
    {"id":"m13-c29","front":"What does the control pilot do?","back":"It carries signals between the charger and the car, including the current limit.","lesson":"m13-l7"},
    {"id":"m13-c30","front":"What is regenerative braking?","back":"The motor acts as a generator to slow the vehicle and recover energy.","lesson":"m13-l8"},
    {"id":"m13-c31","front":"What is brake blending?","back":"The computer combines regeneration and friction braking.","lesson":"m13-l8"},
    {"id":"m13-c32","front":"Why do the brakes of an EV rust?","back":"They are used less, so they are not cleaned by use.","lesson":"m13-l8"},
    {"id":"m13-c33","front":"What transmission do most EVs use?","back":"A single-speed reduction gear.","lesson":"m13-l8"},
    {"id":"m13-c34","front":"What is a power-split hybrid?","back":"A planetary gear set links the engine, generator and motor, and gives a continuously variable ratio.","lesson":"m13-l9"},
    {"id":"m13-c35","front":"What is a P0 mild hybrid?","back":"A belt-driven starter-generator, usually at 48 V, that assists but cannot drive alone.","lesson":"m13-l9"},
    {"id":"m13-c36","front":"Why must you treat a silent hybrid with care?","back":"The engine can start without warning, and the car can move silently, if it is in READY.","lesson":"m13-l9"},
    {"id":"m13-c37","front":"What may an ordinary mechanic do on an EV?","back":"Tyres, 12 V battery, suspension and other work that is not high-voltage.","lesson":"m13-l10"},
    {"id":"m13-c38","front":"How should an EV be towed?","back":"On a flatbed, unless the maker says otherwise.","lesson":"m13-l10"},
    {"id":"m13-c39","front":"What do you do with an isolation fault code?","back":"Refer to a high-voltage specialist.","lesson":"m13-l10"},
    {"id":"m13-c40","front":"Where may you jump start an EV?","back":"Only at the 12 V battery or the points shown in the manual.","lesson":"m13-l10"},
    {"id":"m13-c41","front":"Why lift at the maker's points?","back":"To avoid damaging the battery under the floor.","lesson":"m13-l10"},
    {"id":"m13-c42","front":"How should a damaged EV battery be treated?","back":"Park it outside and away from buildings, put up barriers, tell the maker or a specialist, and expect a delayed fire risk.","lesson":"m13-l11"},
    {"id":"m13-c43","front":"What is the safe way to tow an EV?","back":"On a flatbed, unless the maker's manual says otherwise.","lesson":"m13-l11"},
    {"id":"m13-c44","front":"Storage charge for an EV?","back":"About 30 to 60%, with the 12 V battery kept charged.","lesson":"m13-l11"}
  ]
});
